          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/consistent-hashing.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Consistent Hashing\\ndescription: A distributed systems technique that maps cache keys and nodes onto a circular keyspace, minimizing remapping when nodes are added or removed.\\ntimestamp: 2026-08-17T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Distributed-Systems\\n  - concept/Caching\\n  - pattern/LoadBalancing\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source provides clear technical definition; no contradictions or detailed corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nConsistent hashing is a technique for distributing cache keys across a set of cache nodes in a way that minimizes disruption when nodes are added or removed from the system.\\n\\n## How it Works\\n\\nConsistent hashing maps both cache keys and cache nodes onto the same circular keyspace. When a key is queried, it is hashed onto this circle, and the value is stored on the nearest node in the clockwise direction.\\n\\n### Advantages Over Naive Hashing\\n\\nTraditional hash-based distribution (e.g., \\u0060key_location = hash(key) % num_nodes\\u0060) requires remapping **all keys** when a node is added or removed, because the modulo denominator changes. Consistent hashing avoids this: adding or removing a node only remaps the keys that fall **between the new/removed node and its neighbour**, leaving the rest of the keyspace undisturbed.\\n\\n### Virtual Nodes\\n\\nPhysical cache nodes are often represented multiple times on the ring as **virtual nodes** \\u2014 several ring positions per single physical node. Virtual nodes help even out the load imbalance that a small number of physical nodes would otherwise produce, ensuring more uniform key distribution.\\n\\n## Use Cases\\n\\n- **Distributed caching** (e.g., Memcached, Redis clusters)\\n- **Load balancing** across backend servers\\n- **Peer-to-peer systems** and DHTs (Distributed Hash Tables)\\n- **Sharding** in distributed databases\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
